      "writtenOn": "2017-06-22T01:22:10Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "What does is mean an image is active, committed, valid?  Can all of them be true simultaneously?",
      "revId": "d00f020db7a94c86b51d8509e02a19a135e47fd4",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
    {
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a0e1d27_e5619dd4",
        "filename": "voltha/protos/device.proto",
        "patchSetId": 1
      },
      "lineNbr": 69,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000127
      },
      "writtenOn": "2017-06-22T06:40:49Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "1) By definition, the committed software image is loaded and executed upon reboot of the ONU and/or circuit pack. During normal operation, one software image is always committed, while the other is uncommitted.\n2) By definition, the active software image is one that is currently loaded and executing in the ONU or circuit pack. Under normal operation, one software image is always active while the other is inactive. Under no circumstances are both software images allowed to be active at the same time\n3) By definition, a software image is valid if it has been verified to be an executable code image. The verification mechanism is not subject to standardization; however, it should include at least a data integrity (e.g., CRC) check of the entire code image.\n\nYes, there should be at most one image that is committed, active and valid at the same time.",
      "parentUuid": "9a0e1d27_2ab0828a",
      "revId": "d00f020db7a94c86b51d8509e02a19a135e47fd4",
      "serverId": "2a2bfe1b-c5c2-48ed-9ac8-16438ab24388",
      "unresolved": false
    },
